// Fully standardized to English Core Blueprint & AGENTS.md guidelines

export const c12EngF5HtmlOverview = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, sans-serif; line-height: 1.8; color: #E2E8F0; max-width: 950px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 12px; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n\n  <!-- Chapter Header Badge -->\n  <div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.22), rgba(255, 61, 0, 0.1)); border: 1.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px; margin-bottom: 25px; box-shadow: 0 4px 22px rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.15);\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #FF8A65; color: #000; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 800; padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 6px; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">FLAMINGO &bull; PROSE CHAPTER 5</span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; font-weight: 600;\">CLASS XII ENGLISH CORE</span>\n    </div>\n    <h1 style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 26px; font-weight: 900; margin: 0 0 6px 0; letter-spacing: -0.5px;\">Indigo</h1>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 15px; margin: 0; font-style: italic;\">\n      By Louis Fischer (1896&ndash;1970) &mdash; Excerpted from <i>The Life of Mahatma Gandhi</i>: The Champaran Movement &amp; The Dawn of Non-Violent Civil Disobedience in India\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Quick Glossary & Chapter Dossier Card -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.92); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px; margin-bottom: 25px; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.35);\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 18px; margin: 0 0 12px 0; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;\">\n      <span>📖</span> Quick Glossary &amp; Chapter Dossier: Indigo\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; font-size: 14px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Author:</b> Louis Fischer (Distinguished American Journalist &amp; Gandhi's Biographer)</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Historical Setting:</b> Champaran, Bihar, 1917 (Foothills of Himalayas near Nepal)</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Rajkumar Shukla:</b> Poor, illiterate yet resolute sharecropper who brought Gandhi to Champaran</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Tinkathia System:</b> Colonial contract forcing peasants to grow indigo on 15% (3/20th) of land as rent</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Synthetic Indigo:</b> Chemical German dye that rendered agricultural indigo obsolete, prompting landlord extortion</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Civil Disobedience:</b> First practical triumph in modern India at Motihari courthouse</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">The 25% Refund:</b> Strategic compromise breaking the British planters' prestige and invulnerability</div>\n      <div><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Self-Reliance:</b> Refusal to retain Englishman Charles Freer Andrews as a prop for freedom</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Section 1: Executive Summary & Historical Framework -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 28px 0 16px 0;\">\n    1. Executive Summary &amp; Historical Framework\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    In <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Indigo</b>, acclaimed American biographer <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Louis Fischer</b> reconstructs the historic Champaran Campaign of 1917, which Mahatma Gandhi identified as the pivotal turning point of his life and India’s freedom movement. What began as a modest agrarian appeal by an impoverished, resolute sharecropper named <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Rajkumar Shukla</b> blossomed into India’s inaugural laboratory for non-violent resistance, <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Satyagraha</b>, and <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Civil Disobedience</b>.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    Fischer demonstrates how Gandhi intertwined political protest with deep socio-economic reconstruction. Rather than merely fighting legal battles in remote British courts, Gandhi focused on purging the peasantry of their deep-seated fear of British power, teaching them self-reliance (<b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Atmanirbharta</b>), opening primary schools, promoting hygiene, and proving that ordinary Indians possessed the moral courage to dismantle colonial hegemony.\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- Section 2: Chronological Narrative Flow -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 28px 0 16px 0;\">\n    2. Deep Narrative Progression: The Champaran Satyagraha\n  </h2>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 17px; margin: 20px 0 10px 0;\">(i) The Tenacity of Rajkumar Shukla</h3>\n  <p>\n    At the December 1916 annual session of the Indian National Congress in Lucknow, an emaciated, illiterate sharecropper from Champaran named <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Rajkumar Shukla</b> approached Gandhi. Shukla pleaded with Gandhi to visit Champaran and liberate its tenant farmers from feudal exploitation. Gandhi had never heard of Champaran and was tied to numerous pre-scheduled engagements in Cawnpore (Kanpur) and across India.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    Undeterred, Shukla displayed astonishing tenacity. He followed Gandhi from city to city for months &mdash; to his ashram in Ahmedabad and subsequently to Calcutta &mdash; pleading unceasingly, <i>\"Fix a date!\"</i> Deeply moved by the peasant's sheer resoluteness, Gandhi agreed: <i>\"I have to be in Calcutta on such-and-such a date. Come and meet me and take me from there.\"</i>\n  </p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 17px; margin: 20px 0 10px 0;\">(ii) En Route to Champaran: Patna &amp; Muzaffarpur</h3>\n  <p>\n    Months later, Gandhi and Shukla took the train to Patna, Bihar. Shukla led Gandhi to the residence of lawyer <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Rajendra Prasad</b> (later the first President of India). Because Prasad was away, his orthodox servants mistook Gandhi for another untouchable peasant, making him sit on the bare earth and forbidding him from drawing water from the well lest drops from his bucket pollute the entire reservoir.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    Recognizing that Shukla lacked the legal acumen to present full facts, Gandhi stopped in <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Muzaffarpur</b> to investigate. He was received at midnight by Professor J.B. Kripalani and students, staying with Professor Malkani &mdash; an extraordinary act of courage in an era when government servants feared harboring advocates of \"Home Rule\". When local lawyers called upon Gandhi and boasted of the large fees they charged poor peasants, Gandhi rebuked them sternly: <i>\"I have come to the conclusion that we should stop going to law courts... Where the peasants are so crushed and fear-stricken, law courts are useless. The real relief for them is to be free from fear.\"</i>\n  </p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 17px; margin: 20px 0 10px 0;\">(iii) The Tinkathia System &amp; The Synthetic Indigo Fraud</h3>\n  <p>\n    In Champaran, vast arable estates were owned by British landlords and worked by Indian sharecroppers. Under the ancient <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Tinkathia arrangement</b>, tenants were compelled by contract to plant indigo on 15% (3/20th) of their holdings and surrender the entire harvest as rent to the landlord.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    When Germany developed chemical <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">synthetic indigo</b>, natural indigo farming became commercially unprofitable. British landlords deceitfully coerced illiterate peasants into signing agreements paying huge compensation sums to release them from the 15% mandate. When peasants later discovered the truth about synthetic indigo, they demanded their extorted money back, leading landlords to unleash hired thugs (<i>lathiyals</i>).\n  </p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 17px; margin: 20px 0 10px 0;\">(iv) Motihari Courthouse &amp; The Triumph of Civil Disobedience</h3>\n  <p>\n    Upon arriving in Motihari, the district headquarters, Gandhi was served an official notice by the British Police Superintendent ordering him to quit Champaran immediately. Gandhi signed the summons receipt and boldly wrote on the reverse: <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">\"I will disobey the order.\"</b>\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    By daybreak, the town of Motihari was black with thousands of peasants surrounding the courthouse. The British authorities were entirely paralyzed; their physical power was shattered without Gandhi’s cooperation. Gandhi politely assisted the officials in managing the massive crowd, showing that British might could be challenged by non-violent mass resolve.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    In court, the government prosecutor desperately sought an adjournment. Gandhi objected to the delay and pleaded guilty, explaining he was caught in a <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">\"conflict of duties\"</b>: not to set a bad example as a lawbreaker, but to obey the higher moral law of conscience in rendering humanitarian service to his countrymen. The magistrate postponed judgment, and days later, Lieutenant-Governor Sir Edward Gait dropped all charges: <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Civil Disobedience had triumphed for the first time in modern India</b>.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 17px; margin: 20px 0 10px 0;\">(v) The Commission of Inquiry &amp; The 25% Compromise</h3>\n  <p>\n    Gandhi, joined by eminent lawyers including Rajendra Prasad and Maulana Mazharul Haq, compiled depositions from 10,000 peasants. The evidence of fraud was so overwhelming that the Lieutenant-Governor appointed a formal Commission of Inquiry with Gandhi as the peasants' sole representative.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    When the British planters agreed in principle to make refunds, they anticipated Gandhi would demand 100%. Gandhi surprised them by asking for 50%. The planters offered 25%, hoping to force a stalemate. To their utter bewilderment, Gandhi accepted the 25% refund instantly, breaking the deadlock.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    Gandhi explained his strategic genius: the cash amount was trivial compared to the fact that the British landlords were compelled to surrender their money and their feudal prestige. The peasants saw that British landlords were not above the law, and that they had rights and defenders. Within years, the British planters abandoned their estates, which reverted to the peasants, ending indigo sharecropping permanently.\n  </p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 17px; margin: 20px 0 10px 0;\">(vi) Constructive Social Reform &amp; The Lesson of Self-Reliance</h3>\n  <p>\n    Gandhi refused to leave Champaran after the legal victory. Seeing backwardness and filth in the villages, he opened primary schools in six villages staffed by his son Devadas, Mahadev Desai, Narhari Parikh, and their wives. Kasturbai taught personal hygiene, community sanitation, and self-respect to rural women. A volunteer doctor dispensed simple medicines (castor oil, quinine, and sulphur ointment) for six months.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    When Gandhi’s lawyer friends urged English pacifist <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Charles Freer Andrews</b> to remain in Champaran to aid their struggle, Gandhi vehemently opposed it: <i>\"You think that in this unequal fight it would be helpful if we have an Englishman on our side. This shows the weakness of your heart... You must rely upon yourselves to win the battle.\"</i> Self-reliance was thus established as the soul of Indian Swaraj.\n  </p>\n\n  <!-- Section 3: Literary Devices Master Table -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 28px 0 16px 0;\">\n    3. Literary &amp; Rhetorical Devices Master Table\n  </h2>\n  <div style=\"overflow-x: auto; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14px; text-align: left; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden;\">\n      <thead>\n        <tr style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.25); color: #FFFFFF; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65;\">\n          <th style=\"padding: 12px 14px;\">Literary Device</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 12px 14px;\">Textual Quote / Example</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 12px 14px;\">Thematic Function &amp; Critical Impact</th>\n        </tr>\n      </thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #FF8A65;\">Situational Irony</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px;\">Servants of Rajendra Prasad treat Gandhi as an untouchable peasant.</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Highlights the cruel absurdity of internal caste discrimination against the future Father of the Nation.</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #FF8A65;\">Metaphor</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px;\">\"The morning found the town of Motihari black with peasants.\"</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Compares the overwhelming multitude of farmers to a dark, unstoppable sea confronting colonial authority.</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #FF8A65;\">Moral Paradox</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px;\">\"A conflict of duties: not to be a lawbreaker, yet obeying the voice of conscience.\"</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Exposes the profound philosophical tension between imperial legal edicts and universal human ethics.</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #FF8A65;\">Symbolism</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px;\">The 25% Refund Compromise</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Symbolizes the shattering of British landlord invulnerability and the psychological liberation of the peasantry.</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #FF8A65;\">Antithesis</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px;\">\"The real relief for them is to be free from fear.\"</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px 14px; color: #CBD5E1;\">Contrasts futile institutional litigation in colonial courts with internal psychological emancipation.</td>\n        </tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Section 4: Thematic Pillars -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 28px 0 16px 0;\">\n    4. Core Thematic Pillars &amp; Nationalist Significance\n  </h2>\n  <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 14px;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.08); border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65; padding: 14px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <h4 style=\"color: #FFFFFF; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 15.5px;\">Eradication of Fear</h4>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13.5px;\">Freedom cannot be won in British courts; liberation begins when peasants shed their fear of imperial authority.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.08); border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65; padding: 14px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <h4 style=\"color: #FFFFFF; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 15.5px;\">Strategic Moral Compromise</h4>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13.5px;\">Gandhi accepted 25% refund because breaking the planters' prestige was far more valuable than monetary extraction.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.08); border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65; padding: 14px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <h4 style=\"color: #FFFFFF; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 15.5px;\">Absolute Self-Reliance</h4>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 13.5px;\">Indians must rely on their own strength rather than seeking English allies like Charles Freer Andrews as props.</p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div>\n";

export const c12EngF5HtmlSolutions = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, sans-serif; line-height: 1.8; color: #E2E8F0; max-width: 950px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 12px; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n\n  <!-- Solutions Header Badge -->\n  <div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.22), rgba(255, 61, 0, 0.1)); border: 1.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px; margin-bottom: 25px; box-shadow: 0 4px 22px rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.15);\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #FF8A65; color: #000; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 800; padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 6px; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">QUESTION BANK &bull; CHAPTER 5</span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-size: 13.5px; font-weight: 600;\">CLASS XII ENGLISH CORE</span>\n    </div>\n    <h1 style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 24px; font-weight: 900; margin: 0 0 6px 0;\">Indigo &mdash; Complete Board Solutions</h1>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin: 0;\">\n      Standardized for CBSE &amp; JKBOSE • High-Scoring Extract, Short, Long Thematic &amp; Official NCERT Solutions\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION A: EXTRACT-BASED QUESTIONS -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 28px 0 16px 0;\">\n    SECTION A: Extract-Based Questions (16 Questions | 4 Extracts)\n  </h2>\n\n  <!-- Extract 1 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-style: italic; margin-top: 0; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Read the extract given below and answer the questions that follow:</b><br/>\n      \"The morning found the town of Motihari black with peasants. They did not know Gandhi’s record in South Africa. They had merely heard that a Mahatma who wanted to help them was in trouble with the authorities. Their spontaneous demonstration, in thousands, around the courthouse was the beginning of their liberation from fear of the British.\"\n    </p>\n    \n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15px;\">Q1: Why did thousands of peasants gather in Motihari?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> They had heard that a Mahatma who came to champion their rights was in trouble with the colonial authorities and summoned to court.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15px;\">Q2: What was the peasants' knowledge regarding Gandhi's earlier achievements?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> They knew nothing of his campaigns against racism in South Africa; they simply recognized him as a saint standing up for them.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15px;\">Q3: Why was this demonstration deemed a historic turning point in the independence struggle?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> It marked the spontaneous shedding of their age-old, paralyzing fear of the British colonial administration.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15px;\">Q4: What metaphor is used to describe the multitude of gathered farmers?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> <i>\"Motihari black with peasants\"</i>, describing the sheer density and dark sea of people.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Extract 2 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-style: italic; margin-top: 0; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Read the extract given below and answer the questions that follow:</b><br/>\n      \"He explained that the amount of the refund was less important than the fact that the landlords had been obliged to surrender part of the money, and with it, part of their prestige. Therefore, as far as the peasants were concerned, the planters had behaved as lords above the law. Now the peasant saw that he had rights and defenders. He learned courage.\"\n    </p>\n    \n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15px;\">Q5: What percentage refund had Gandhi accepted that astonished his followers?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> A 25% refund offered by the British planters.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15px;\">Q6: Why was the monetary refund secondary in importance according to Gandhi?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Because forcing the British planters to refund even a quarter of the extorted sum compelled them to surrender their prestige and status above the law.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15px;\">Q7: What profound psychological transformation took place among the sharecroppers?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> They realized they had legal rights and defenders, instilling courage and eradicating subservient fear.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15px;\">Q8: What long-term outcome followed this compromise in Champaran?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> British planters abandoned their estates within a few years, and indigo sharecropping was permanently abolished.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Extract 3 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-style: italic; margin-top: 0; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Read the extract given below and answer the questions that follow:</b><br/>\n      \"Gandhi told them that they were not to set a bad example as a lawbreaker, but on the other hand to render the humanitarian and national service for which he had come. He had disregarded the order not out of lack of respect for lawful authority, but in obedience to the higher law of our being, the voice of conscience.\"\n    </p>\n    \n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15px;\">Q9: What moral dilemma or 'conflict of duties' did Gandhi articulate here?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Balancing the duty of not setting a bad precedent as a lawbreaker against the sacred obligation of rendering humanitarian service to oppressed peasants.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15px;\">Q10: What was the 'higher law' to which Gandhi pledged supreme obedience?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The higher law of being &mdash; the inner voice of conscience and moral justice.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15px;\">Q11: Which official order had Gandhi disregarded in Champaran?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The Police Superintendent's formal eviction notice ordering him to quit the district of Champaran immediately.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15px;\">Q12: What was the magistrate's immediate reaction to Gandhi's statement?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The magistrate postponed delivering judgment for two hours and released Gandhi without bail.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Extract 4 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; font-style: italic; margin-top: 0; line-height: 1.7;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Read the extract given below and answer the questions that follow:</b><br/>\n      \"Gandhi vehemently opposed the proposal. 'You think that in this unequal fight it would be helpful if we have an Englishman on our side. This shows the weakness of your heart. The cause is just and you must rely upon yourselves to win the battle. You should not seek a prop in Mr. Andrews because he happens to be an Englishman.'\"\n    </p>\n    \n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15px;\">Q13: Who was Mr. Andrews referred to in this extract?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Charles Freer Andrews, an English pacifist missionary and devoted follower of Mahatma Gandhi.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15px;\">Q14: Why did Gandhi's lawyer friends want Mr. Andrews to remain in Champaran?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> They believed that having an influential Englishman advocate on their side would give them a strategic advantage against British authorities.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15px;\">Q15: Why did Gandhi rebuke them for seeking a 'prop'?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> He declared that seeking an English ally revealed psychological weakness and lack of faith in their own strength and justice.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15px;\">Q16: What core nationalist virtue did Gandhi teach through this decision?</b><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Self-reliance (<i>Atmanirbharta</i>) and independent moral courage.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION B: SHORT ANSWER QUESTIONS -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 30px 0 16px 0;\">\n    SECTION B: Short Answer Questions (3 Marks Each | 40&ndash;50 Words)\n  </h2>\n\n  <!-- Q17 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q17: Why is Rajkumar Shukla described as being extraordinarily 'resolute'?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Despite Gandhi's tight commitments across Cawnpore, Ahmedabad, and Calcutta, Shukla never wavered. He shadowed Gandhi patiently for months, waiting outside his ashram and pleading unceasingly until Gandhi consented to fix a date for Champaran.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q18 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q18: How was Gandhi treated by the servants at Rajendra Prasad’s house in Patna?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The servants recognized Shukla as an impoverished peasant and assumed Gandhi was another untouchable farmer. They made him sit on the bare floor and barred him from drawing water from the well lest bucket droplets pollute the water.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q19 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q19: Why did Gandhi decide to go to Muzaffarpur before proceeding directly to Champaran?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Shukla was illiterate and lacked the capacity to provide comprehensive legal documentation. Gandhi paused in Muzaffarpur to meet lawyers and professors like J.B. Kripalani to gather detailed, verified facts regarding agrarian tenancy conditions.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q20 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q20: Why did Gandhi severely rebuke the lawyers of Muzaffarpur?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Gandhi reprimanded the lawyers for extorting exorbitant fees from destitute, terrorized sharecroppers. He declared that British courts were useless where people were crushed by fear; the primary priority was freeing peasants from fear.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q21 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q21: What was the commercial agreement (Tinkathia system) between landlords and tenants?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Under long-term leases, British planters compelled tenant sharecroppers to plant indigo on 15% (3/20th) of their holdings and surrender the entire indigo harvest as rental payment to the landlords.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q22 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q22: How did the discovery of synthetic indigo lead to landlord deceit and conflict?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> When Germany developed cheap synthetic indigo, natural indigo cultivation became unprofitable. British landlords deceitfully demanded cash compensation from illiterate sharecroppers to release them from the 15% agreement, unleashing thugs against those who resisted.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q23 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q23: What \"conflict of duties\" did Gandhi articulate before the Motihari court?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Gandhi stated he was torn between not setting a bad example as a lawbreaker, and rendering humanitarian and national service to oppressed sharecroppers in obedience to the higher moral law of conscience.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q24 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q24: Why did Gandhi accept a 25% refund rather than demanding a full 100%?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Gandhi recognized that the monetary amount was secondary to the psychological victory of shattering planter supremacy. Surrendering 25% compelled the British to forfeit their prestige, teaching the peasants courage and self-worth.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q25 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q25: What social and educational reforms did Gandhi implement in Champaran?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Gandhi opened primary schools in six villages, engaged teachers like Devadas and Mahadev Desai, mobilized Kasturbai to teach hygiene and sanitation to rural women, and stationed a volunteer doctor with essential medicines.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q26 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q26: Why did Gandhi oppose keeping Charles Freer Andrews in Champaran?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Gandhi argued that relying on an Englishman in an unequal fight betrayed psychological weakness. Because their cause was just, Indians had to depend entirely upon their own internal strength, making self-reliance fundamental.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q27 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q27: How did the Champaran campaign influence prominent lawyers like Rajendra Prasad?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> The lawyers initially planned to return home if Gandhi went to prison. However, seeing an outsider ready to risk imprisonment for peasants shamed them into realizing that abandoning the cause would be cowardly betrayal, pledging to follow Gandhi to jail.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q28 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q28: What long-term agrarian changes occurred in Champaran after Gandhi’s intervention?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Within a few years of the settlement, British landlords abandoned their extensive estates, which reverted to the peasant cultivators. The oppressive indigo sharecropping system was permanently abolished.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION C: LONG ANSWER QUESTIONS -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 30px 0 16px 0;\">\n    SECTION C: Long Answer Questions (6 Marks Each | 120&ndash;150 Words)\n  </h2>\n\n  <!-- Q29 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 22px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16.5px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-weight: 800;\">Q29: Why is the Champaran episode considered the definitive turning point in Gandhi’s life and the Indian national movement?</h4>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Introduction &amp; Agrarian Origin:</b><br/>\n        The Champaran movement of 1917 began not as an act of political agitation, but as an empathetic effort to alleviate the immediate economic exploitation of thousands of illiterate sharecroppers.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Historic Achievements:</b><br/>\n        First, Champaran served as the inaugural national victory of <b>Civil Disobedience and Satyagraha</b>. When Gandhi refused to obey the eviction order at Motihari, thousands of peasants rallied around the courthouse, paralyzing British administration and compelling the Lieutenant-Governor to drop all charges. Second, it shattered the myth of British invincibility, showing sharecroppers that planters were not lords above the law.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Nationalist Significance:</b><br/>\n        Most importantly, Gandhi demonstrated that political liberty is hollow without grassroots socio-economic reconstruction. By combining non-violent resistance with village schools, sanitation, and self-reliance, Champaran became the blueprint for India’s freedom struggle.\n      </p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q30 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 22px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16.5px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-weight: 800;\">Q30: How did Gandhi demonstrate astute statesmanship and strategic wisdom during the 25% refund settlement?</h4>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">The Tactical Impasse:</b><br/>\n        Armed with 10,000 documented depositions proving systematic landlord fraud, Gandhi held total moral supremacy. The British planters, expecting Gandhi to demand a 100% refund, offered 25% as an obstinate bargaining tactic to force a stalemate.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Strategic Acceptance:</b><br/>\n        To the utter astonishment of his legal team, Gandhi accepted the 25% refund instantly, breaking the deadlock. Gandhi explained that the financial percentage was trivial compared to the psychological surrender forced upon the British. For over a century, British planters had acted as despots above the law. Compelling them to return money publicly shattered their prestige.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Long-Term Triumph:</b><br/>\n        The peasants witnessed that they possessed legal rights and defenders. Within a few years, the planters abandoned their estates, proving Gandhi’s compromise was a masterstroke of statesmanship.\n      </p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q31 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 22px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16.5px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-weight: 800;\">Q31: How did Gandhi weave together political activism, social reform, and the virtue of self-reliance in Champaran?</h4>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Holistic Nationalism:</b><br/>\n        Gandhi’s political vision was intimately tied to the day-to-day welfare of impoverished masses. He refused to depart Champaran after the legal settlement, recognizing that legal rights mean nothing amid crushing illiteracy and filth.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Constructive Village Program:</b><br/>\n        Gandhi opened primary schools across six villages, recruiting volunteer teachers including Devadas, Mahadev Desai, and Narhari Parikh. Kasturbai taught personal hygiene and sanitation to rural women. A volunteer doctor dispensed simple medical remedies for six months to combat chronic skin and digestive ailments.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">The Injunction of Self-Reliance:</b><br/>\n        Furthermore, when local lawyers sought to retain English pacifist Charles Freer Andrews as an ally, Gandhi strongly objected, arguing that relying on a foreigner betrayed internal weakness. True Swaraj required Indians to rely upon their own moral strength.\n      </p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q32 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 22px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16.5px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-weight: 800;\">Q32: Analyze the dramatic events at the Motihari courthouse that marked the birth of mass Civil Disobedience.</h4>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">The Ultimatum &amp; Peasant Mobilization:</b><br/>\n        When Gandhi endorsed the Police Superintendent's notice with the words \"I will disobey the order\", he was summoned to court. Overnight, word spread across the countryside; by dawn, the town of Motihari was black with thousands of peasants.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">The Paralysis of Imperial Power:</b><br/>\n        The British administrative machinery was utterly paralyzed. Officials stood helpless until Gandhi politely stepped forward to regulate the crowd, proving that British might could no longer intimidate unarmed masses. In court, Gandhi pleaded guilty, citing a higher moral duty of conscience.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">The Retreat of the Empire:</b><br/>\n        Unnerved by the peaceful defiance, the magistrate released Gandhi without bail, and days later, the Lieutenant-Governor dropped all proceedings, marking the first nationwide triumph of Civil Disobedience.\n      </p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q33 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 22px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16.5px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-weight: 800;\">Q33: How did Gandhi transform the mindset of the elite Bihari lawyers from mercenary advocates to committed freedom fighters?</h4>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Initial Mercenary Mindset:</b><br/>\n        When Gandhi first arrived in Muzaffarpur, the local lawyers briefed him while openly boasting about the large fees they extracted from impoverished sharecroppers. Gandhi severely rebuked them for exploiting destitute peasants.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">The Moral Awakening:</b><br/>\n        Later, when Gandhi faced the prospect of prison in Motihari, he asked the lawyers what they would do if he were arrested. A senior lawyer answered that they would simply return home. Gandhi challenged them: what would happen to the injustice done to the sharecroppers?\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Commitment to Sacrifice:</b><br/>\n        Withdrew for private consultations, Rajendra Prasad and his colleagues realized that if a total outsider was prepared to go to jail for their peasants, their retreat would be shameful betrayal. They returned to Gandhi and pledged to follow him to prison.\n      </p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q34 -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 22px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16.5px; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-weight: 800;\">Q34: Explain the profound significance of Gandhi’s statement: \"The real relief for them is to be free from fear.\"</h4>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Critique of Colonial Legalism:</b><br/>\n        Gandhi perceived that taking peasant grievances to British law courts was futile because the judicial system was designed to protect imperial property rights, not peasant welfare.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0 0 10px 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Psychological Enslavement:</b><br/>\n        The root problem was not merely the Tinkathia contract, but the terror instilled by British landlords, police, and magistrates. As long as peasants lived in dread of landlord thugs and prison, legal verdicts could never secure genuine freedom.\n      </p>\n      <p style=\"margin: 0;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Internal Emancipation as Freedom:</b><br/>\n        By congregating by thousands outside the courthouse, defying eviction notices, and recording bold testimonies, the peasants shed their psychological shackles. Once fear vanished, colonial exploitation collapsed from within.\n      </p>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION D: NCERT TEXTBOOK EXERCISES -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 800; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 6px; margin: 30px 0 16px 0;\">\n    SECTION D: NCERT Textbook Exercises (Think As You Read &amp; Understanding The Text)\n  </h2>\n\n  <!-- Q35 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q35: Why do you think Gandhi considered the Champaran episode to be a turning point in his life?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Champaran proved to Gandhi that ordinary, unlettered peasants could be mobilized into an invincible non-violent force capable of challenging British imperialism. It established Civil Disobedience as a viable political tool and inaugurated his leadership on the national stage.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q36 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q36: How was Gandhi able to influence the lawyers of Bihar? Give instances.</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Gandhi shamed them for charging heavy fees to impoverished sharecroppers. Later, when Gandhi faced imprisonment, the lawyers recognized that if an outsider was prepared to go to jail for peasants, their retreat would be shameful betrayal, prompting them to pledge to follow him to prison.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q37 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q37: What was the attitude of average Indians in smaller localities towards advocates of 'Home Rule'?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> In smaller localities, average Indians lived in mortal fear of British retaliation and were afraid to display sympathy to advocates of Home Rule. Therefore, harboring Gandhi in Professor Malkani's home was considered an act of extraordinary courage.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- Q38 -->\n  <div style=\"margin-bottom: 20px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.65); padding: 14px 16px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #FF8A65;\">\n    <h4 style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px; margin: 0 0 8px 0; font-weight: 700;\">Q38: How do we know that ordinary people contributed significantly to the freedom movement?</h4>\n    <p style=\"margin: 0; color: #CBD5E1; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold;\">✓ Answer:</span> Unlettered peasants like Rajkumar Shukla initiated the movement; thousands spontaneously encircled the Motihari courthouse; nearly 10,000 farmers recorded depositions; and rural women attended sanitation classes, demonstrating that mass participation drove India’s liberation.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n</div>\n";

